WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:02.532
Here is a whole number.

2
00:00:02.632 --> 00:00:03.692
It's finite.

3
00:00:03.742 --> 00:00:04.855
It's exact.

4
00:00:05.055 --> 00:00:09.689
And unless mathematics itself is broken,
no proof will ever tell us what it is.

5
00:00:10.139 --> 00:00:14.324
To see why, we need the simplest
computer imaginable, and a game.

6
00:00:17.874 --> 00:00:22.877
A Turing machine is an endless tape of
zeros, a head, and a tiny rule book.

7
00:00:23.127 --> 00:00:27.607
At each step, it reads the cell under the
head, writes a bit, moves left or right,

8
00:00:27.647 --> 00:00:28.829
and switches state.

9
00:00:28.879 --> 00:00:31.587
Until it hits the one rule that says: halt.

10
00:00:32.637 --> 00:00:36.553
In 1962, Tibor Radó turned this into a game.

11
00:00:36.653 --> 00:00:39.729
With n states, build the
machine that runs the longest.

12
00:00:39.769 --> 00:00:41.546
But it must eventually stop.

13
00:00:41.896 --> 00:00:43.949
Some machines halt almost at once.

14
00:00:43.989 --> 00:00:45.367
Most run forever.

15
00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:50.115
The longest run that still ends is
called BB(n): the busy beaver number.

16
00:00:51.265 --> 00:00:54.058
One state: the best you can do is one step.

17
00:00:54.158 --> 00:00:55.589
Two states: six.

18
00:00:55.689 --> 00:00:57.350
Three states: twenty-one.

19
00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.711
Four states: one hundred and seven.

20
00:00:59.761 --> 00:01:02.971
A value not proven until 1983.

21
00:01:04.121 --> 00:01:05.523
Then, five states.

22
00:01:05.673 --> 00:01:06.574
In 1989,

23
00:01:06.614 --> 00:01:11.270
Heiner Marxen and Jürgen Buntrock found

24
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:16.165
a machine that runs for 47,176,870 steps.

25
00:01:16.465 --> 00:01:17.774
And then, it stops.

26
00:01:19.124 --> 00:01:21.606
Look inside, and it's doing arithmetic.

27
00:01:21.656 --> 00:01:26.108
It carries a number, and keeps replacing
it with about five-thirds of itself.

28
00:01:26.158 --> 00:01:30.367
Fourteen hops later, the number
is 2 mod 3, and the machine halts.

29
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:36.622
Proving that nothing beats it took until
2024, when an online collaboration,

30
00:01:36.662 --> 00:01:38.717
the Busy Beaver Challenge,

31
00:01:38.757 --> 00:01:44.437
sorted all 181 million essentially
different five-state machines into halters

32
00:01:44.477 --> 00:01:45.544
and non-halters.

33
00:01:45.594 --> 00:01:48.553
Every step, checked by a
computer proof assistant.

34
00:01:49.903 --> 00:01:51.016
Six states.

35
00:01:51.066 --> 00:01:51.659
In 2022,

36
00:01:51.699 --> 00:01:55.343
Pavel Kropitz found a machine that runs for

37
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:58.463
more than ten tetrated to fifteen steps:

38
00:01:58.503 --> 00:02:00.660
a power tower of fifteen tens.

39
00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.897
In 2025, a contributor known as mxdys found

40
00:02:04.937 --> 00:02:08.130
one that runs longer than a tower of twos,

41
00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:13.852
whose height is a tower of twos,
whose height is a tower of ten twos.

42
00:02:14.602 --> 00:02:17.902
Radó proved this explosion is unavoidable:

43
00:02:17.942 --> 00:02:21.339
BB eventually outgrows
every computable function.

44
00:02:22.689 --> 00:02:24.692
But size is not what stops us.

45
00:02:24.842 --> 00:02:28.929
Hiding among the six-state machines
is one nicknamed Antihydra.

46
00:02:28.979 --> 00:02:31.565
Strip away the tape,
and this is all it does.

47
00:02:32.015 --> 00:02:33.138
Start with 8.

48
00:02:33.188 --> 00:02:36.833
Multiply by three halves,
rounding down, again and again.

49
00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:39.020
Each even number scores two points.

50
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.806
Each odd number loses one.

51
00:02:40.856 --> 00:02:43.816
If the score ever goes
negative, the machine halts.

52
00:02:44.566 --> 00:02:47.909
It has been run for over 270 billion rounds.

53
00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:51.982
The score just keeps drifting upward,
like a coin rigged in its favor.

54
00:02:52.532 --> 00:02:54.374
It almost surely runs forever.

55
00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:59.232
But proving it means predicting the parity
of numbers multiplied by three halves,

56
00:02:59.272 --> 00:03:04.239
again and again: a cousin of the Collatz
problem, about which Paul Erdős said:

57
00:03:04.439 --> 00:03:07.447
“Mathematics may not be
ready for such problems.”

58
00:03:07.997 --> 00:03:11.686
Until someone solves it,
BB(6) remains out of reach.

59
00:03:13.236 --> 00:03:14.593
And here's the twist.

60
00:03:14.693 --> 00:03:19.876
Goldbach's conjecture, that every even
number above 2 is a sum of two primes,

61
00:03:19.916 --> 00:03:24.353
fits inside a 25-state machine
that hunts for a counterexample.

62
00:03:24.453 --> 00:03:28.167
The Riemann hypothesis fits inside 744.

63
00:03:28.717 --> 00:03:33.423
So if you knew BB(25), you could
settle Goldbach by brute force.

64
00:03:33.473 --> 00:03:35.299
Run the machine that many steps.

65
00:03:35.349 --> 00:03:37.974
If it hasn't halted by then, it never will.

66
00:03:39.524 --> 00:03:44.184
Now, build a machine that searches every
proof in Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory,

67
00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:47.840
the foundation of modern
mathematics, for a contradiction.

68
00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.929
It halts only if mathematics
contradicts itself.

69
00:03:51.479 --> 00:03:55.139
If set theory could pin down
BB for that machine's size,

70
00:03:55.179 --> 00:04:00.070
it could prove the machine never halts,
which means proving its own consistency.

71
00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:01.861
And Gödel showed it can't.

72
00:04:03.011 --> 00:04:07.032
In 2016, the first explicit machine beyond

73
00:04:07.072 --> 00:04:10.478
set theory's reach had 7,910 states.

74
00:04:10.678 --> 00:04:12.916
The record has been falling ever since.

75
00:04:13.066 --> 00:04:14.736
748.

76
00:04:14.836 --> 00:04:16.566
745.

77
00:04:16.666 --> 00:04:18.307
549.

78
00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:22.558
And, in a claim from 2025: 432.

79
00:04:23.908 --> 00:04:28.760
So somewhere between six states and four
hundred thirty-two, mathematics runs out.

80
00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.412
Aaronson suspects it's as close as twenty.

81
00:04:32.362 --> 00:04:37.476
Out past that edge, every busy beaver is
still a perfectly ordinary whole number.

82
00:04:38.426 --> 00:04:40.529
We will simply never prove which one.
